"user\",\n      \"content\": \"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}\"\n    }\n  ]","parameterType":"JSON"},{"key":"max_tokens","value":"150","parameterType":"Number"}],"Body":{"model":"gpt-4o","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"Summarize the following passage, which describes a conspiracy belief, in a single sentence. Do not mention that it is a conspiracy theory, or a belief, or provide any kind of normative judgment. Merely accurately describe the content of the conspiracy theory in a single sentence, framing it in a manner that suggests the conspiracy is TRUE. So, even if the writing is arguing that the earth is not flat, you would say 'the earth is flat'). Frame it as a true assertion (regardless of whether evidence actually supports the belief). If the statement is already short, no need to change it very much. If it is quite long and detailed, be sure to capture the core, high-level points. If the respondent equivocates or couches their language in uncertainty, be sure not to include this equivocation in your summary. Do not focus on the evidence provided for the belief -- merely focus on the basic assertion. That is, use the evidence and justifications they might provide only for context. For your information, the passage was written by a participant in an online academic survey in response to the following two questions: < Q1 Asked participants explicitly to briefly describe a conspiracy theory they feel genuinely uncertain about (they could see it either being true or false). > < Q2 Asked participants to discuss specific reasons for their partial belief, explicitly mentioning both evidence for belief and skepticism. > Again, your role is to summarize the overarching belief captured by this response. Your summary will be shown to the participant, who will be asked to rate their agreement with the statement; their belief in the summary will be used as a psychometrics-style item in a scientific experiment."},{"role":"user","content":"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}"}],"max_tokens":150},"ContentType":"application\/json","Headers":[{"key":"Authorization","value":"Bearer REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY_ROTATE_ME"}],"ResponseMap":[{"key":"choices.0.message.content","value":"conRestatement"}],"FireAndForget":false,"SchemaVersion":0,"StringifyValues":true},{"Type":"WebService","FlowID":"FL_166","URL":"https:\/\/api.openai.com\/v1\/chat\/completions","Method":"POST","RequestParams":[],"EditBodyParams":[{"key":"model","value":"gpt-4o"},{"key":"messages","value":"[\n    {\n      \"role\": \"system\",\n      \"content\": \"Describe the conspiracy being discussed in a sentence or two. Do not provide any kind of normative judgment. Merely accurately describe the conspiracy theory at hand (framing it in a way so that another reader would understand what the conspiracy theory claims and is about. Do not focus unduly the evidence provided for the belief -- mostly focus on the basic assertion. Try to ignore whether the person who authored the text you'll be reading supports or opposes the conspiracy. Your job is just to capture the conspiracy claim. So, if they oppose the truth of the conspiracy, that is fine, but still return a description of the conspiracy that they're claiming is not true. For your context, the text you will be reading was written by a participant in an online academic survey in response to the following two questions: < Q1 Asked participants explicitly to briefly describe a conspiracy theory they feel genuinely uncertain about (they could see it either being true or false). > < Q2 Asked participants to discuss specific reasons for their partial belief, explicitly mentioning both evidence for belief and skepticism. >\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": \"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}\"\n    }\n  ]","parameterType":"JSON"},{"key":"max_tokens","value":"150","parameterType":"Number"}],"Body":{"model":"gpt-4o","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"Describe the conspiracy being discussed in a sentence or two. Do not provide any kind of normative judgment. Merely accurately describe the conspiracy theory at hand (framing it in a way so that another reader would understand what the conspiracy theory claims and is about. Do not focus unduly the evidence provided for the belief -- mostly focus on the basic assertion. Try to ignore whether the person who authored the text you'll be reading supports or opposes the conspiracy. Your job is just to capture the conspiracy claim. So, if they oppose the truth of the conspiracy, that is fine, but still return a description of the conspiracy that they're claiming is not true. For your context, the text you will be reading was written by a participant in an online academic survey in response to the following two questions: < Q1 Asked participants explicitly to briefly describe a conspiracy theory they feel genuinely uncertain about (they could see it either being true or false). > < Q2 Asked participants to discuss specific reasons for their partial belief, explicitly mentioning both evidence for belief and skepticism. >"},{"role":"user","content":"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}"}],"max_tokens":150},"ContentType":"application\/json","Headers":[{"key":"Authorization","value":"Bearer REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY_ROTATE_ME"}],"ResponseMap":[{"key":"choices.0.message.content","value":"conSummary"}],"FireAndForget":false,"SchemaVersion":0,"StringifyValues":true},{"Type":"WebService","FlowID":"FL_167","URL":"https:\/\/api.openai.com\/v1\/chat\/completions","Method":"POST","RequestParams":[],"EditBodyParams":[{"key":"model","value":"gpt-4o"},{"key":"messages","value":"[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"system\",\n    \"content\": \"You are classifying responses from participants in an online survey about conspiracy theories. Participants answered two questions:\\n\\n(Q1) Asked participants explicitly to briefly describe a conspiracy theory they feel genuinely uncertain about (they could see it either being true or false).\\n(Q2) Asked participants to discuss specific reasons for their partial belief, explicitly mentioning both evidence for belief and skepticism.\\n\\nYour task is to determine whether the participant\u2019s responses reflect ambivalent or uncertain belief in a conspiracy theory or a closely related idea.\\n\\nUse the everyday (folk) concept of a conspiracy theory, not a strict academic definition. Borderline or loosely related scenarios, like cryptid sightings, paranormal phenomena, or secretive cover-ups, are acceptable and should be classified as TRUE if uncertainty or ambivalence is at least plausible.\\n\\nClassify as TRUE if:\\n- The response clearly or implicitly indicates uncertainty or ambivalence about the described theory.\\n- The participant describes a specific conspiracy theory (or something closely related) without explicitly stating their level of certainty. Given the original survey instructions explicitly requested a conspiracy theory they're unsure about, presume implicit ambivalence and classify as TRUE.\\n\\nClassify as FALSE if:\\n- The participant explicitly expresses firm belief or complete rejection of the conspiracy theory without any sign of uncertainty or ambivalence.\\n - The participant says that they don't really hold conspiracy beliefs or seem to misread the question or similar \\n Classify as INVALID if \/n - The participant\u2019s responses are nonsensical, random, clearly inattentive, irrelevant, or obviously off-topic. \/n - Responses appear auto-generated, robotic, spam-like, include characters or script a human is unlikely to use, or otherwise clearly indicate lack of genuine engagement.\\n\\nIf ambivalence is unclear, default to classifying responses as TRUE, favoring false positives. Only respond with TRUE or FALSE or INVALID\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"user\",\n    \"content\": \"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}\"\n  }\n]","parameterType":"JSON"},{"key":"max_tokens","value":"150","parameterType":"Number"}],"Body":{"model":"gpt-4o","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are classifying responses from participants in an online survey about conspiracy theories. Participants answered two questions:\n\n(Q1) Asked participants explicitly to briefly describe a conspiracy theory they feel genuinely uncertain about (they could see it either being true or false).\n(Q2) Asked participants to discuss specific reasons for their partial belief, explicitly mentioning both evidence for belief and skepticism.\n\nYour task is to determine whether the participant\u2019s responses reflect ambivalent or uncertain belief in a conspiracy theory or a closely related idea.\n\nUse the everyday (folk) concept of a conspiracy theory, not a strict academic definition. Borderline or loosely related scenarios, like cryptid sightings, paranormal phenomena, or secretive cover-ups, are acceptable and should be classified as TRUE if uncertainty or ambivalence is at least plausible.\n\nClassify as TRUE if:\n- The response clearly or implicitly indicates uncertainty or ambivalence about the described theory.\n- The participant describes a specific conspiracy theory (or something closely related) without explicitly stating their level of certainty. Given the original survey instructions explicitly requested a conspiracy theory they're unsure about, presume implicit ambivalence and classify as TRUE.\n\nClassify as FALSE if:\n- The participant explicitly expresses firm belief or complete rejection of the conspiracy theory without any sign of uncertainty or ambivalence.\n - The participant says that they don't really hold conspiracy beliefs or seem to misread the question or similar \n Classify as INVALID if \/n - The participant\u2019s responses are nonsensical, random, clearly inattentive, irrelevant, or obviously off-topic. \/n - Responses appear auto-generated, robotic, spam-like, include characters or script a human is unlikely to use, or otherwise clearly indicate lack of genuine engagement.\n\nIf ambivalence is unclear, default to classifying responses as TRUE, favoring false positives. Only respond with TRUE or FALSE or INVALID"},{"role":"user","content":"Question 1 Response: ${q:\/\/QID69\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}, Question 2 Response: ${q:\/\/QID70\/ChoiceTextEntryValue}"}],"max_tokens":150},"ContentType":"application\/json","Headers":[{"key":"Authorization","value":"Bearer REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY_ROTATE_ME"}],"ResponseMap":[{"key":"choices.0.message.content","value":"isEquivocal"}],"FireAndForget":false,"SchemaVersion":0,"StringifyValues":true},{"Type":"Branch","FlowID":"FL_261","Description":"New Branch","BranchLogic":{"0":{"0":{"LogicType":"EmbeddedField","LeftOperand":"isEquivocal","Operator":"EqualTo","RightOperand":"INVALID","_HiddenExpression":false,"Type":"Expression","Description":"<span class=\"ConjDesc\">If<\/span> <span class=\"LeftOpDesc\">isEquivocal<\/span> <span class=\"OpDesc\">Is Equal to<\/span> <span class=\"RightOpDesc\"> INVALID <\/span>"},"Type":"If"},"Type":"BooleanExpression"},"Flow":[{"Type":"EndSurvey","FlowID":"FL_262","EndingType":"Advanced","Options":{"Advanced":"true","SurveyTermination":"Redirect","EOSRedirectURL":"https:\/\/samplicio.us\/s\/ClientCallBack.aspx?RIS=20&RID=${e:\/\/Field\/rid}"}}]}]}]}]}]},{"Type":"Standard","ID":"BL_ePNbZOXPITDPHCu","FlowID":"FL_32","Autofill":[]},{"Type":"Branch","FlowID":"FL_171","Description":"New Branch","BranchLogic":{"0":{"0":{"LogicType":"EmbeddedField","LeftOperand":"condition","Operator":"EqualTo","RightOperand":"treatment_bunk","_HiddenExpression":false,"Type":"Expression","Description":"<span class=\"ConjDesc\">If<\/span